<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kościuszko Uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kosciuszko Uprising was a revolt led by Tadeusz Kosciuszko in 1794 against Russian and Prussian domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a>. Despite early successes, it was crushed by Russian and Prussian forces. Its defeat led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a> of 1795, which ended the Commonwealth's existence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising.jpg" alt="Kościuszko Uprising" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BorysMapping · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ko%C5%9Bciuszko_uprising.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Great Sejm of 1788 to 1792 adopted a new constitution on 3 May 1791, at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s neighbours were occupied with wars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> opposed it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">Magnates</a> who had opposed the reform, among them Franciszek Ksawery Branicki and Stanisław Szczęsny Potocki, formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/targowica-confederation">Targowica Confederation</a> and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> to intervene. Russian armies entered the Commonwealth on 18 May 1792, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a> capitulated. The Grodno Sejm of 1793 annulled the constitution and accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a>, by which Russia took 250,000 square kilometres and Prussia 58,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko">Tadeusz Kościuszko</a>, who had not lost a battle in the campaign, resigned his commission and planned an uprising from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 12 March 1794 General Antoni Madaliński refused an order to demobilise and led his cavalry brigade towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. Kościuszko, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, announced the general uprising in Kraków's town square on 24 March and took command of all Polish forces. He defeated a Russian corps at Racławice on 4 April. An uprising led by Jan Kiliński drove the Russian garrison out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 17 April, and on 23 April Jakub Jasiński began one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. On 7 May Kościuszko issued the Proclamation of Połaniec, which partly abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and gave civil liberty to peasants, though the nobility boycotted it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> entered the war on 10 May. Kościuszko was defeated at Szczekociny on 6 June, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> was besieged from 13 July until early September. On 10 October he was wounded and captured at Maciejowice. On 4 November Alexander Suvorov's troops stormed Praga, a suburb of Warsaw, and by one Russian estimate about 20,000 people, soldiers and civilians, were killed. Tomasz Wawrzecki surrendered on 16 November near Radoszyce.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 150,000 men took up arms during the eight months, and the Polish rebels lost 27,000 men and the Russians 4,080. The following year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a>, annexing the rest of the country. Reforms easing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> were revoked, and the Commission of National Education was abolished. In the Russian partition thousands of noble families who had supported the uprising lost their estates. The uprising and the Proclamation of Połaniec are seen as the start of modern political thought and of the leftist movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kościuszko Uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kościuszko Uprising</b> of 1794, an attempt to mobilise the whole nation including the peasantry, was crushed by Russian and Prussian forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1796 Józef Wybicki, a leading moderate émigré politician, proposed Polish Legions within the French Revolutionary Army and persuaded General Jan Henryk Dąbrowski, a hero of the 1794 <b>Kościuszko Uprising</b>, to put the plan to the French Directory.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li><li><blockquote>His speech is considered the start of the <b>Kościuszko Uprising</b>, whose defeat led to the Third Partition of 1795.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
